// Specialised: alliances, ceasefires and the rest of the diplomacy panel.
//
// TWO RULES CONSTRAIN EVERY WAITING PAGE HERE, and both were learned by
// writing pages that could not be satisfied.
//
// 1. Wait only on what the PLAYER can do. Never on the alliance existing --
//    that is the other country's decision, and in the tutorial that country
//    is a hand-written turtle entitled to say no. A page waiting on somebody
//    else's answer hangs forever with no way out.
//
// 2. Only ONE request may be pending toward a given country at a time: the
//    panel greys every other entry while one is in flight (anyDiploPending in
//    Game_Render.cpp, which is per target). So a lesson cannot ask for four
//    requests in a row -- it asks for one, has the player end the turn, and
//    asks for the next.
//
// The country used throughout is Verrick, because Kestrel is at war with us
// from the first turn and a country you are at war with offers exactly one
// entry: Request Ceasefire.

:: anchor=left size=20 pad=26 enter=type speed=44 line=1.4

@Signals Officer
Diplomacy. All of it lives in one list, and the list belongs to a
§**country**§ -- so you reach it by clicking somebody's ground.

I will wait at each step. Nothing here is fast.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.relations gate=1 until=view:relations
First, the §**relations**§ view. It paints the map by who is at war with whom,
who is allied, and who has guaranteed whom.
---
@Signals Officer
Look at it before you start anything. Half the wars people lose here were
against three countries rather than one -- and all three were on this map
before the first shot.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province gate=1 until=selected:atpeace
Now click a province belonging to §**Verrick**§, across the water.

Not Kestrel: we are already at war with them, and a country you are at war
with offers exactly one thing. We will come to that one -- and until you pick
somebody we are at peace with, this page will simply keep waiting.
---
@Signals Officer
Their panel has grown a list. What is in it depends entirely on what the two
of you already are to each other -- you never choose from every option, only
from the ones your current relationship allows.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province gate=1 until=filed:request_nap
The mildest thing on it is a §**non-aggression pact**§: a promise not to
attack, and nothing more.

Ask for one. It costs nothing to ask.
---
@Signals Officer
Filed -- and now look at the list. §**Everything else has gone grey**§, and the
entry you just used has turned into §**Cancel**§.

One request at a time to one country. That is not a bug you have hit, it is
the rule: they are answering the last thing you said, and until they do your
only move is to take it back.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=button.end_turn gate=1 until=turns:1
So end the turn. The request goes out, they answer, and the list comes back.

They may refuse. That is their business and not a fault in you.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province gate=1 until=filed:request_alliance
Verrick again. An §**alliance**§ is the next step up -- not merely a promise
not to attack, but a promise to join in.

Ask for one.
---
@Signals Officer
With an ally, one more entry appears: §**Call to Arms**§, which drags them
into a war you are already fighting.

They can refuse that too. An ally who refuses twice is an ally on paper, and
worth about as much.
---
@Signals Officer
The two you have not used: a §**guarantee**§ is the lopsided one -- you
promise to defend somebody who has promised you nothing. Small countries
collect them; large ones hand them out to buy quiet.

And §**trade**§ is goods for money every turn, until somebody cancels. It is
the only entry that makes both of you richer, which is why it is the one
people forget is there.
---
@Signals Officer
Once a pact is signed, watch what leaves the list: §**Declare War
disappears**§. You have to break the pact first, in public, where everyone
sees you do it.

That is what a pact is for. It is not a shield, it is a witness.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.relations gate=1 until=view:relations
And at §**war**§ the whole list collapses to one entry: §**Request
Ceasefire**§. It comes back carrying terms -- who stops, and who keeps what.

They accept when they are losing and refuse when they are not. No amount of
asking changes which of those is true.
---
@Signals Officer
Kestrel is in red on this map, and that is the list they would show you.
---
@Signals Officer
That is diplomacy. Anything else?
